ecmaOS: SSIZE_MAX is the largest ssize_t, which on this target is a pointer-sized int (_Addr), not a long: musl's
LONG_MAX holds on its other targets, where the two are one size, but here long is 8 bytes and ssize_t 4, so a check
against it never failed (open_memstream's seek bound, gnulib's regex size limits).

--- i/include/limits.h
+++ w/include/limits.h
@@ -50,7 +50,7 @@
 #define IOV_MAX 1024
 #define SYMLOOP_MAX 40
 #define WORD_BIT 32
-#define SSIZE_MAX LONG_MAX
+#define SSIZE_MAX __INTPTR_MAX__
 #define TZNAME_MAX 6
 #define TTY_NAME_MAX 32
 #define HOST_NAME_MAX 255
